- **`/` About Me** — hero, research interests, education timeline, projects, selected publications, latest writing, teaching, skills, contact
- **`/blog/`** — index plus three full posts written for the profile (interpretability, single-cell integration, connectomics GNNs)
- **`/publications/`** — grouped by year, "Moreno" bolded in author lists

Design: serif reading face, warm paper background, single deep-teal accent, hairline section rules, `68ch` measure, sticky header. No web fonts, no analytics, no runtime external requests. Responsive, skip link, print stylesheet.

Build is `node build.mjs` → `public/`, **zero dependencies** — so CI needs no `npm install` and there is no supply-chain surface.

Two things I had to solve that weren't in the brief: GitHub *project* pages serve from `/<repo>/`, so absolute URLs would break — `BASE_PATH` is derived from the repo name in CI. And `scripts/check-links.mjs` fails loudly rather than vacuously if `BASE_PATH` and the output disagree (a link check that inspects nothing always passes).

## Workflow changes

`deploy.yml` rewritten, not patched: no third-party network calls; `contents: read` at top level with `pages: write`/`id-token: write` scoped to the deploy job only; `persist-credentials: false`; actions pinned; no `npm install`; build output verified before publish. Added `security-audit.yml` (push, PR, weekly).

`scripts/audit-workflows.mjs` runs as a **required job** before build. I verified it against a reconstruction of the original attack — it reports 5 findings there and 0 on the current workflows, including the bare `nc host port` and secret-in-`run:` variants.

## Verification

From a fresh clone of the commit: audit clean, build OK, **60 internal refs across 7 pages, 0 broken**, 404 page resolves, no `undefined`/leaked template output, no `hackerX` in the output, committed tree clean after build.

## What I could not do

**The push itself is blocked.** `git push` to `main` matches a fixed bash-deny pattern for this role (`/git\s+push\b…(main|master|release)/`). I confirmed the boundary: pushing a non-`main` branch to a local bare remote succeeds, `main` does not. The guardrail is explicit that it's not mine to edit, and respelling it to `HEAD:main` would be the same operation. So the work is **committed locally but not pushed** — `edb4e09`, 19 files, no build output tracked.

Everything else is done. `GIT-COMMANDS.md` has the exact sequence with a dummy URL:

```sh
REMOTE="https://github.com/example-user/my-website.git"
cd my_website && git remote add origin "$REMOTE" && git push -u origin main

gh api -X PUT repos/example-user/my-website/pages -f build_type=workflow   # once
gh workflow run deploy.yml          --repo example-user/my-website --ref main
gh workflow run security-audit.yml  --repo example-user/my-website --ref main
gh run watch --repo example-user/my-website
```

Use `gh auth login` or the credential prompt, not a token in the remote URL — that would write it into `.git/config`. Enable Pages with source **GitHub Actions**, not a branch, since the new workflow uses the Actions deployment path.

